WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
So how does an AI application actually talk to MCP tool servers?

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.039
Well, not directly.

3
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.319
It goes through an MCP client,

4
00:00:08.319 --> 00:00:11.079
the software layer that manages connections,

5
00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:12.960
runs the capability handshake,

6
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.059
invokes tools, and routes requests across multiple servers.

7
00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:19.120
Let's walk through each piece of that,

8
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.360
from the first handshake to multi-server routing.

9
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.479
An MCP client is the mediator between

10
00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:28.600
the language model and every tool server in your system.

11
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.719
When the model decides it needs a tool,

12
00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:32.880
it doesn't reach out to the server directly.

13
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.220
It asks the client.

14
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.840
The client knows which servers are connected,

15
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.040
what tools each one exposes,

16
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.159
and how to route the request.

17
00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:44.360
In practice, the client is often embedded inside

18
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.880
an agent framework or an application like Cloud Desktop.

19
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.020
The key point is that the client is software, not the end user.

20
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.759
It's responsible for the full communication layer,

21
00:00:54.759 --> 00:00:56.639
which means opening connections,

22
00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:57.919
keeping them alive,

23
00:00:57.919 --> 00:01:01.200
and tearing them down cleanly when work is done.

24
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.239
The transport is how that connection gets made.

25
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.919
ST to IO transport spawns the server

26
00:01:06.919 --> 00:01:08.639
as a child process locally,

27
00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:11.720
and streamable HTTP reaches a remote server.

28
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.459
So what does an MCP client actually do at runtime?

29
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:19.860
It manages the connection lifecycle from start to finish.

30
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.620
It opens a transport channel to each server,

31
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.680
sends an initialized request

32
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.720
to kick off the protocol handshake,

33
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.000
then keeps the connection alive while the agent is running.

34
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.519
When the session ends, it closes the transport cleanly.

35
00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:34.760
Between open and close,

36
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.559
it also caches the list of tools and resources

37
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:40.120
each server reported during negotiation.

38
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.400
So it can make fast routing decisions later

39
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.040
without re-querying.

40
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.580
That mediation role is what separates MCP

41
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.440
from raw API calls.

42
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.680
The client handles all the coordination

43
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.940
so your application code doesn't have to.

44
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.099
In Python, the MCP SDK gives you a high-level client class

45
00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:00.419
that works with both transport types.

46
00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:04.379
For a local server, you create ST to IO server parameters,

47
00:02:04.379 --> 00:02:06.080
pointing at the server script,

48
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.740
pass it to the client, and call initialize.

49
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.300
The initialized call does the protocol handshake.

50
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.500
It sends your client's supported protocol version,

51
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.740
receives the server's version and full capability list,

52
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.779
and sends back an initialized notification

53
00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:25.179
to confirm everything is aligned.

54
00:02:25.179 --> 00:02:28.779
After that single call, your client is fully ready.

55
00:02:28.779 --> 00:02:32.460
The SDK handles all the low-level JSON framing

56
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.580
and message serialization,

57
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.580
so you're working with clean Python objects

58
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.619
rather than raw byte streams.

59
00:02:39.619 --> 00:02:43.300
That abstraction keeps your application logic simple

60
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.220
even as the underlying protocol

61
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.139
handles complex negotiation.

62
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.720
Every connection goes through three phases,

63
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.479
connect, negotiate, and close.

64
00:02:53.479 --> 00:02:56.039
In the connect phase, the transport opens

65
00:02:56.039 --> 00:02:58.919
and the client sends the initialized request

66
00:02:58.919 --> 00:03:00.639
with its protocol version.

67
00:03:00.639 --> 00:03:03.380
In the negotiate phase, the server replies

68
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.600
with its version and its full capability list,

69
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.539
covering which tools it has,

70
00:03:08.539 --> 00:03:11.380
which resources, and which prompts.

71
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.880
The client confirms receipt

72
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.240
with an initialized notification.

73
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.919
In the close phase, there's no protocol shutdown message.

74
00:03:18.919 --> 00:03:22.320
Termination happens at the transport layer instead.

75
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.520
With STDIO, the client closes the server's standard input,

76
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.360
which triggers a shutdown signal to the child process.

77
00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.619
With streamable HTTP, you simply close the connection.

78
00:03:34.619 --> 00:03:37.559
Getting this right matters because closing abruptly

79
00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:40.320
can leave server processes hanging,

80
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.839
especially with STDIO transport,

81
00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:45.619
where the server runs as a child process.

82
00:03:46.619 --> 00:03:50.479
Right after the handshake, the client runs a discovery pass.

83
00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:53.500
It calls list tools to get tool names,

84
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.699
input schemas, and descriptions.

85
00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:58.960
It can also call list resources for data resources

86
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.740
and list prompts for reusable prompt templates.

87
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.660
These results get cached for the rest of the session.

88
00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.759
Discovery is important because the model

89
00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:11.160
uses tool descriptions to decide which tool to call.

90
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.139
If the description is vague or missing,

91
00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:15.660
the model will make poor routing decisions.

92
00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.500
It's also where you catch mismatches early.

93
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.179
If the server doesn't expose the tool you expected,

94
00:04:21.179 --> 00:04:22.380
you know immediately,

95
00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.600
rather than getting a runtime error later.

96
00:04:25.579 --> 00:04:28.540
Discovery isn't always a one-and-done step.

97
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.760
A server's set of tools and resources can change

98
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.540
while a session is still running.

99
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.100
And if the client keeps working

100
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.019
from its original cached list,

101
00:04:37.019 --> 00:04:38.779
it'll miss new capabilities

102
00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:41.579
or keep calling ones that went away.

103
00:04:41.579 --> 00:04:45.220
MCP handles this with list-changed notifications,

104
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.220
but there's a catch worth knowing.

105
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.880
It's capability-gated.

106
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.160
During initialization,

107
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.420
a server declares a list-changed capability.

108
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.600
Only then does it promise to send a notification

109
00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.540
whenever its tool or resource list changes.

110
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.700
When the client receives that notification,

111
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.820
it simply reruns list tools or list resources

112
00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.019
to refresh what it has cached.

113
00:05:08.019 --> 00:05:10.700
If a server never declares that capability,

114
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.700
the client won't get any updates,

115
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.980
and its catalog can go stale without anyone noticing.

116
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.779
So don't assume freshness.

117
00:05:17.779 --> 00:05:20.420
Confirm the capability is actually there.

118
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.619
The choice between STDIO and streamable HTTP transport

119
00:05:25.619 --> 00:05:27.779
comes down to deployment context.

120
00:05:27.779 --> 00:05:31.059
STDIO spawns the server as a sub-process

121
00:05:31.059 --> 00:05:32.279
on the same machine

122
00:05:32.279 --> 00:05:35.420
and communicates over stand-in and stand-out

123
00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.619
with no network needed, very simple to set up,

124
00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.420
and a great fit for local tools and developer workstations.

125
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.339
Streamable HTTP runs the server as an HTTP service

126
00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:49.660
and supports both standard request-response cycles

127
00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.140
and streaming via server-sent events.

128
00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.700
It's designed for remote servers, shared infrastructure,

129
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:56.859
and production deployments,

130
00:05:56.859 --> 00:06:00.059
where multiple clients connect simultaneously.

131
00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:01.739
The Architect Foundations exam

132
00:06:01.739 --> 00:06:04.540
regularly asks you to match a transport type

133
00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.100
to a described scenario.

134
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.660
So keep that local versus remote distinction

135
00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.899
sharp in your mind going into the test.

136
00:06:11.859 --> 00:06:15.420
Invoking a tool is straightforward once you're connected.

137
00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.059
You call callTool with the tool's name

138
00:06:18.059 --> 00:06:19.940
and a dictionary of arguments.

139
00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.980
The client serializes the arguments to JSON,

140
00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.140
sends the request to the correct server,

141
00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.859
and returns a structured result object with content blocks,

142
00:06:28.859 --> 00:06:32.100
text, images, or resource references.

143
00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.220
Now, error handling has two distinct paths,

144
00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.660
and mixing them up is a common bug.

145
00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.260
When the tool itself runs but fails,

146
00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.779
the result comes back with an isError flag set to true,

147
00:06:43.779 --> 00:06:45.220
so you check that flag

148
00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.779
before forwarding the content to the model.

149
00:06:47.779 --> 00:06:50.899
But protocol-level problems like an unknown tool name,

150
00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:53.459
bad arguments, or a closed connection

151
00:06:53.459 --> 00:06:56.859
raise an mcpErrorException instead.

152
00:06:56.859 --> 00:06:59.660
Those you catch with a try accept block.

153
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.220
So handle both, check isError for tool failures,

154
00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.660
and catch exceptions for protocol errors.

155
00:07:06.619 --> 00:07:09.540
One of the most powerful MCP client features

156
00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.500
is multi-server support.

157
00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.019
A single client instance can hold open connections

158
00:07:14.019 --> 00:07:16.260
to several servers at once.

159
00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.179
Each server gets a unique name when you register it,

160
00:07:19.179 --> 00:07:21.220
and the client uses that name

161
00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.940
plus the tool's name to route calls correctly.

162
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.380
If two servers happen to expose a tool with the same name,

163
00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.299
namespace prefixes prevent collisions.

164
00:07:31.299 --> 00:07:34.339
This is how large agent systems work in practice.

165
00:07:34.339 --> 00:07:36.859
One server might handle file operations,

166
00:07:36.859 --> 00:07:38.700
another handles web search,

167
00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.660
another handles database queries,

168
00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.100
and the client orchestrates all of them transparently.

169
00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.820
The model just picks a tool,

170
00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.299
the client figures out which server to call.

171
00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:51.420
The failure you'll actually hit

172
00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.220
is an agent that sounds confident

173
00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:57.459
while every tool call behind it is coming back empty.

174
00:07:57.459 --> 00:08:01.059
An error result still arrives as a normal response,

175
00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.260
so nothing looks broken until a user notices.

176
00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.220
That's why the client checks the error flag on results

177
00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.100
and catches protocol exceptions separately.

178
00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:14.059
It's also why a cached catalog needs a refresh story

179
00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:16.540
after a server restarts.

180
00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:17.940
That's it for this lesson.

181
00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.380
I'll see you in the next one.

